> You can run it with a partial phylogeny.  Your results will be better with a full phylogeny, but of course even a complete phylogeny will be missing all of the extinct groups.  BioGeoBEARS is a form of ancestral state estimation applied to biogeography (a special kind of character state), so the logic about phylogenetic diversity analyses isn't really applicable.  Basically, you want as much data as you can practically gather to get the best results, but of course all studies are limited by what is available.
>
> My 2014 SysBio paper has a big section simulating the effect of missing extinct species, basically it doesn't hurt ancestral range estimation too much as long as the rates of change are low.
